[#Binary Tree](https://leetcode.com/tag/binary-tree/) #Neetcode150

we know in BST, left subtree have the smaller value from their parent's value.

In this question, we want Kth smallest value in a BST.

We can use DFS inorder since BST follows inorder left -> root -> right

1) If node is null, return undefined
2) explore left subtree, `let left = dfs(node.left)`
3) `If left !== undefined`, it means the left subtree already found the kth smallest node, so return it immediately.

4) Otherwise, visit the current node : count++
5) If k === count, then we have kth smallest node, so return node's value.
6) Otherwise, explore the right subtree: `return dfs(node.right)`

```js
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * function TreeNode(val, left, right) {
 *     this.val = (val===undefined ? 0 : val)
 *     this.left = (left===undefined ? null : left)
 *     this.right = (right===undefined ? null : right)
 * }
 */
/**
 * @param {TreeNode} root
 * @param {number} k
 * @return {number}
 */
var kthSmallest = function(root, k) {
            let count = 0
        const dfs = (node) => {


            if(!node){
                return //undefined
            }

            //if left subtree have the answer, return it
            let left = dfs(node.left)
            if(left !== undefined) return left

            count+= 1

            if(k === count) return node.val

            return dfs(node.right)

        }

        return (dfs(root))

};
```

[3345. Smallest Divisible Digit Product I](https://leetcode.com/problems/smallest-divisible-digit-product-i/description/)

[#Math](https://leetcode.com/tag/math/)
[#Enumeration](https://leetcode.com/tag/enumeration/)
#StreakQ

In this question, we have to try from numbers equal or more than n, so that the product of digits of n, is divisible by t.
We have to find the smallest n, which satifies this.

So, set out `product = 1`, and for each num, we find the product of the digits and if its divisble by t then we return the number, or else recusivly call the question by (n+1, t)

```js
/**
 * @param {number} n
 * @param {number} t
 * @return {number}
 */
var smallestNumber = function(n, t) {

        let num = n
        let product = 1
        while(num > 0 ){
            let lastDigit = num % 10
            product *= lastDigit
            num = Math.floor(num / 10)
        }

        if(product % t === 0){
            return n 
        }

        return smallestNumber(n+1, t)


};
```

[1372. Longest ZigZag Path in a Binary Tree](https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-zigzag-path-in-a-binary-tree/description/)

[#Dynamic Programming](https://leetcode.com/tag/dynamic-programming/)
[#Tree](https://leetcode.com/tag/tree/)
[#Depth-First Search](https://leetcode.com/tag/depth-first-search/)
[#Binary Tree](https://leetcode.com/tag/binary-tree/)
[#DP on Trees](https://leetcode.com/tag/dp-on-trees/)

dir represents the direction of the previous edge.

1) If the current move is opposite to dir, we extend the current ZigZag (length + 1).
2) If the current move is the same as dir, the ZigZag breaks, so we restart from length 1.
3) Since the root has no previous direction, we start DFS twice—once assuming the previous direction was left and once assuming it was right.

```js
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * function TreeNode(val, left, right) {
 *     this.val = (val===undefined ? 0 : val)
 *     this.left = (left===undefined ? null : left)
 *     this.right = (right===undefined ? null : right)
 * }
 */
/**
 * @param {TreeNode} root
 * @return {number}
 */
var longestZigZag = function(root) {

    let maxLength = 0

    // 0 -> left, 1 -> right
    const zigzag = (node, dir, length) => {
        if(!node) return 
        maxLength = Math.max(length, maxLength)

        //args
        //going left, 
        //will arrive at node.left with left move ,  
        //if prev move was right, length+1
        zigzag(node.left, 0, dir === 1 ? length + 1 : 1)

        //args
        //going right, 
        //will arrive at node.right with right move , 
        //if prev move was left, length+1
        zigzag(node.right, 1, dir === 0 ? length + 1 : 1)
    }

    //checking both possibilties

    //assuming, arrived at root with left move
    zigzag(root, 0, 0)

    ///assuming,  arrived at root with right move
    zigzag(root, 1, 0)

    return maxLength
};
```

[105. Construct Binary Tree from Preorder and Inorder Traversal](https://leetcode.com/problems/construct-binary-tree-from-preorder-and-inorder-traversal/description/)

